//
// Regression (loft#751) — a `vector<integer>` handed to a `vector<u8>` parameter
// was accepted with no diagnostic, and the callee then read each 8-byte element
// as eight 1-byte ones.  `text_from_bytes([72, 105] as vector<integer>)` answered
// `"H "` instead of `"Hi"`; `write_bytes` turned a 24-byte payload into a
// ~200 KB file whose own reader then refused it.
//
// In a REGISTER an `integer` and a `u8` are one type, which is why `is_equal`
// compares two scalar integers by kind alone.  In a VECTOR they are not: the
// element width IS the stride.  So the acceptance was `is_equal`'s vector arm
// asking its element question with the register rule.
//
// ⚠ The element COUNT agreed throughout — a 3-element `vector<integer>` read as
// bytes still reports `len() == 3`, because the count is stored — so every check
// written in terms of the length passed while only the bytes were wrong.  That
// is what made it silent, and it is why the guard below asserts BYTES.
//
// The same mismatch spelled as a LITERAL was already refused
// (`d: vector<u8> = [1, 300]`), so the two spellings of one mistake disagreed.
//
// Dual guard, the 432b pattern: `@EXPECT_ERROR` matches the compile error, and
// if the refusal ever regresses the file compiles and RUNS `main`, where the
// reinterpreted bytes are not `"Hi"` and the assert fires.

fn take(v: vector<u8>) -> text { text_from_bytes(v) }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: expected vector<u8>, got vector<integer>
fn main() {
  // The near side of the boundary: a `vector<u8>` built from integer LITERALS
  // is typed at the target width element-wise and must stay legal.
  a: vector<u8> = [72, 105];
  assert(take(a) == "Hi", "vector<u8> argument must still work");

  // THE CASE: same values, declared `vector<integer>`, so the elements are
  // 8 bytes wide and reading them as bytes yields `72, 0,0,0,0,0,0,0, 105, …`.
  b: vector<integer> = [72, 105];
  assert(take(b) == "Hi",
    "a vector<integer> argument to a vector<u8> parameter must be refused at compile time");
}
